<div class="learn-box">
**What you will learn**

Select records, set inclusive time windows, and reduce them to first, last, count, ever, closest, any and all values.
</div>

This chapter uses the synthetic practice connection from [Getting started](book-00-start.html).
Run its sections in order. Each chapter starts from a fresh practice connection;
all the outputs below come from real dsOMOP and dsOMOPClient 2.10.4 calls.
The numbers describe teaching data, not clinical findings.

## Define the records once

An *event* describes which records the server should read. This practice measurement
has numeric values recorded in kilograms. Concept IDs here are vocabulary inputs;
they are not patient identifiers.

```{r measurement}
measure <- omop_event("measurement", concepts = 950002L,
  value = "value_as_number", where = omop_where(unit_concept_id == 9529L))
class(measure)
```

The unit predicate keeps values recorded in that unit; it does not convert units.
Use an `omop_unit()` conversion when your study needs conversion.

## Set a public time window

A *window* is an inclusive range of days around an *anchor*, the reference date.
A fixed date works even when the population has no index event.

```{r window}
history <- omop_window(-366, 0, anchor = "2021-01-01")
class(history)
```

This includes 2020-01-01 through 2021-01-01. Both endpoints count.
An omitted window on an advanced reducer reads all history; a simple-layer
shortcut usually defaults to history through the index. Choose explicitly.

## First and last values

A *reducer* turns many records into one value per person or episode.

```{r first-last}
first <- omop_variable(event = measure,
  reducer = omop_reduce("first_value", window = history))
last <- omop_variable(event = measure,
  reducer = omop_reduce("last_value", window = history))
class(last)
```

These select by date, source and record key, not by the size of the value.
By default they skip missing values. `missing = "keep"` chooses the row first,
so the selected row can give a missing value. With no records, both give `NA`.

## Count and ever

```{r count-ever}
n <- omop_variable(event = measure,
  reducer = omop_reduce("count", window = history, distinct = "day"))
ever <- omop_variable(event = measure,
  reducer = omop_reduce("binary", window = history))
class(n)
```

Count measures distinct measurement days here; `distinct = "row"` counts records.
Ever is 1 when any record exists and 0 otherwise. Neither requires a nonmissing
numeric value. In a defined empty window both count and ever are zero.

## Any and all test the values

```{r any-all}
any_high <- omop_variable(event = measure,
  reducer = omop_reduce("any", window = history,
    predicate = omop_where(value >= 70)))
all_high <- omop_variable(event = measure,
  reducer = omop_reduce("all", window = history,
    predicate = omop_where(value >= 70)))
class(any_high)
```

Ever tests whether a record exists. Any and all test a condition on its values.
A missing value makes its comparison unknown; a true value settles any, and a
false value settles all. Otherwise an unknown remains missing. Empty windows
give any = 0 and all = NA: without measurements there is no all-value result.

## Assign the columns privately

```{r reducers-recipe}
reducers <- omop_recipe(variables = list(first = first, last = last,
  days = n, ever = ever, any_high = any_high, all_high = all_high),
  outputs = omop_output("people", format = "wide", grain = "person"))
stopifnot(recipe_explain(reducers, conns = conns)$selected == "v2")
print(recipe_execute(reducers, out = c(people = "D_reducers"), conns = conns))
```

Only the symbol mapping returns. Request one approved aggregate at a time:

```{r first-result}
dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_reducers$first", datasources = conns)
```

This summarises the first nonmissing measurement per person.

```{r last-result}
dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_reducers$last", datasources = conns)
```

The later value changes the last-value summary. Missing people remain in the roster.

```{r days-result}
dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_reducers$days", useNA = "no", datasources = conns)
```

These are people grouped by their number of measurement days, not event counts pooled directly.

```{r ever-result}
dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_reducers$ever", useNA = "no", datasources = conns)
```

The people with no measurements have ever = 0.

```{r any-result}
dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_reducers$any_high", useNA = "always", datasources = conns)
```

A known high measurement is enough for any, even alongside an unknown value.

```{r all-result}
dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_reducers$all_high", useNA = "always", datasources = conns)
```

All distinguishes unknown comparisons from an empty set of records.

## Value at the index

Now let the first visit set a different anchor for each person. *Value at index*
means closest within a chosen tolerance; it need not occur on the index day.

```{r at-index}
at_index <- omop_study(omop_event("visit_occurrence"), outputs = "baseline",
  baseline = list(weight = omop_value_at_index("measurement", 950002L,
    tolerance = c(-30, 0), unit = 9529L)))
stopifnot(recipe_explain(at_index, conns = conns)$selected == "v2")
print(recipe_execute(at_index, out = c(baseline = "D_at_index"), conns = conns))
```

The tolerance excludes later measurements. The shortcut uses `closest_value`;
its default tolerance would include 90 days before and 30 days after.

```{r at-index-result}
dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_at_index$weight", datasources = conns)
```

Only people with a visit enter this indexed study. If a required anchor is missing,
a window is undefined and every reducer gives `NA`, including count and ever.

## Recap

An event chooses records, a window chooses time, and a reducer answers one question. Empty records and an undefined anchor are different.

## Try it

Add a count of measurement rows and compare it with the count of distinct days.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```{r solution}
row_count <- omop_recipe(variables = list(rows = omop_variable(event = measure,
  reducer = omop_reduce("count", window = history, distinct = "row"))),
  outputs = omop_output("people", format = "wide", grain = "person"))
stopifnot(recipe_explain(row_count, conns = conns)$selected == "v2")
print(recipe_execute(row_count, out = c(people = "D_row_count"), conns = conns))
dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_row_count$rows", useNA = "no", datasources = conns)
```
They agree in this fixture because there is at most one measurement per person per day. That is a property of these data, not of the reducers.

</details>
